<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabalega</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1853–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chwa II Kabalega, Kabarega</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabalega was omukama (king) of Bunyoro in western Uganda from 1870 to 1899. He built a standing army, the abarusura, revived Bunyoro's power, reconquered Toro in 1876 and resisted the advance of Buganda. From 1891 he fought the British and their Ganda allies in a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war until his capture in 1899, after which he was exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>. Uganda honours him as a hero of resistance.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chwa II Kabalega was born on 18 June 1853. When Omukama Kamurasi Kyebambe IV died in 1869, two royal candidates, Kabalega and Kabigumire, could not agree on the succession, and a destructive war broke out in the kingdom of Kitara. Such contests were meant to be brief, but this one dragged on until the clan leaders and the dead king's brother, Prince Nyaika, ordered Kabalega, who had the upper hand, to respect Bunyoro's laws of succession and end the fighting. He came to power in 1870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As king, Kabalega set out to develop his realm through trade, above all through the Kibiro Saltworks. He centralised power by relying on the army rather than the traditional nobility, and his soldiers, the abarusura, had a reputation for ruthlessness. He also built diplomatic ties with other states. He resisted both Egyptian and British expansion, and his forces defeated Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> in 1872.</p><p>On 1 January 1894 the British declared war on Bunyoro, and a war of nearly five years followed. At the height of the British offensive Kabalega went into hiding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a> under the protection of Chief Awich Abok of Payira, and from there he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyangire-revolt">Nyangire rebellion</a> against the colonial power. He was captured on 9 April 1899 together with his ally Kabaka Mwanga II of Buganda and exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, where he spent 24 years, writing letters and petitions to various leaders against colonial rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kabalega died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a> on 6 April 1923, on his way home from exile, and was buried at Mparo near Hoima. In 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murchison-falls">Murchison Falls</a>, in Murchison Falls National Park, as Kabalega Falls in his honour. On 8 June 2009 Museveni declared him a national hero of Uganda, and in 2010 the Omukama of Bunyoro-Kitara founded the Most Honourable Order of Omukama Chwa II Kabalega.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabalega of Bunyoro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Firearms reached the kingdom only in small numbers, through Arab and Swahili traders working from Karagwe and Buganda, and never transformed its forces as they transformed Bunyoro's under <b>Kabalega</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1870s and 1880s Buganda faced a revived Bunyoro under Omukama <b>Kabalega</b>, whose armed forces resisted its advance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kabalega</b> was captured in 1899 together with Mwanga, the deposed king of Buganda, a long-time rival who had become his ally; the British exiled both men to the Seychelles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Bunyoro's king <b>Kabalega</b> reconquered Toro in 1876, but in 1891 Frederick Lugard of the Imperial British East Africa Company restored the Toro dynasty under Kasagama.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-toro">Kingdom of Toro</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Imperial British East Africa Company withdrew, Britain declared a protectorate over Buganda in 1894 and extended it to Bunyoro, Toro, Ankole and Busoga in the following years, after defeating <b>Kabalega</b> of Bunyoro.</blockquote><a href="/state/uganda-protectorate">Uganda Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bunyoro. Died in Jinja, in what is now <a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=0.44,33.21&amp;year=1898" aria-label="Jinja on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5472 2147 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kabalega"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5922 2417h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5922 2417h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=0.44,33.21&amp;year=1898">What was here in 1898? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
